{{!-- custom-institution-guide: /institutions/guide/.
     Restructured 2026-08-12 on the StoryBrand frame at Ian's direction.
     The organization is the hero; Mere Orthodoxy is the guide. The
     three-step plan IS the Push feature, and the administrator and
     member sections are the plan in detail.

     NO ITALICS on this page, per Ian 2026-08-19. The theme's house style
     italicises display headings by wrapping the phrase in an em tag
     (.section-heading em and .membership-support .page-hero-headline em
     are the only rules that turn italics on), so italics here are
     entirely a markup decision — the CSS sets none by itself. Do not
     reintroduce em tags when editing copy. UI labels that need to stand
     out use strong, as Push/Pushed and Sign in do.

     ONE .included band, not four. That class carries 96px of band-edge
     padding and is meant to be used once, as membership-body.hbs does;
     stacking it produced ~744px of dead terracotta.

     Light variants on every .eyebrow and .section-heading inside the
     band. Without them the eyebrow renders at 1.03:1 and is invisible. --}}

<style>
  /* Links inside the band. The theme's default link colour is terracotta,
     which is also .included's background, so an unstyled link here is
     invisible. --color-white keeps this on the token set; 4px offset keeps
     it on the spacing grid. */
  .institution-guide .included .benefit-item a,
  .institution-guide .capstone a {
    color: var(--color-white);
    text-decoration: underline;
    text-underline-offset: 4px;
    text-decoration-thickness: 1px;
  }
  .institution-guide .included .benefit-item a:hover,
  .institution-guide .capstone a:hover {
    text-decoration-thickness: 2px;
  }
  /* screen.css has no global :focus-visible rule, so without this these
     links have no keyboard indicator at all on a dark background. */
  .institution-guide .included .benefit-item a:focus-visible,
  .institution-guide .capstone a:focus-visible {
    outline: 2px solid var(--color-white);
    outline-offset: 4px;
    text-decoration-thickness: 2px;
  }
</style>

<main class="membership membership-support institution-guide">

  {{!-- 1. The hero, and what they want. --}}
  <section class="page-hero">
    <div class="container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Organizational Membership</p>
      <h1 class="page-hero-headline">Give your people something worth being formed by.</h1>
      <p class="page-hero-sub">Every congregation and every faculty is being formed by something. This is how you get a say in what.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  {{!-- 2. The problem, and 3. the guide. Empathy first, then authority. --}}
  <section class="form-section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-intro">
        <h2 class="section-heading">The problem is not that your people aren't reading.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="institutional-overview">
        <div class="institutional-point">
          <h3>They are being formed by something</h3>
          <p>Everyone in your congregation, your faculty, or your staff is being catechized this week by someone. The only question is by whom, and whether anyone chose it on their behalf.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="institutional-point">
          <h3>You cannot outpublish the feed</h3>
          <p>Most churches and schools have no staff writer, no editor, and no publishing arm. Producing serious formation content is a full-time vocation, and you already have one of those.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="institutional-point">
          <h3>So borrow ours</h3>
          <p>We exist to form Christians in the church to participate in the culture for the common good. Twenty years of essays, two podcasts, a quarterly journal, and writers who take the tradition seriously. There are four of us. We know what a small budget looks like.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  {{!-- 4. The plan. Three steps, which is also the Push feature. --}}
  <section class="included">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-intro">
        <p class="eyebrow eyebrow-light">The plan</p>
        <h2 class="section-heading section-heading-light">Choose what your people read.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="benefit-grid">
        <div class="benefit-item">
          <span class="benefit-numeral">I</span>
          <h3>Cover everyone at once</h3>
          <p>Access runs on your email domain, so anyone at your organization gets a full membership without you assigning a single seat.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="benefit-item">
          <span class="benefit-numeral">II</span>
          <h3>Push what matters</h3>
          <p>When you are signed in as an administrator, a <strong>Push</strong> button appears on every essay and on every episode of Mere&nbsp;Fidelity and Christians Reading Classics. One click sends it to your people. The button changes to <strong>Pushed</strong> so you can see what you have already sent, and clicking again takes it back down.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="benefit-item">
          <span class="benefit-numeral">III</span>
          <h3>They read it together</h3>
          <p>What you push appears under <a href="/dashboard/institution/">Organization Reading</a> on each member's dashboard, credited to you rather than to us. It reads as something their church or school handed them, because it is.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="capstone">
        <p class="capstone-body">This is the difference between buying a subscription and running a media ministry. A pastor can put the essay on forgiveness in front of the elders before Thursday's meeting. A dean can hand the whole faculty one piece on the university's vocation and know they all have it. A campus minister can build a semester of reading without a coursepack, a budget line, or a permissions request.</p>
        <p class="capstone-body">You are not asking anyone to find the good stuff. You are choosing it for them, which is what shepherding attention has always meant.</p>
      </div>

      {{!-- The plan in detail. Practical content, unchanged in substance. --}}
      <div class="section-intro">
        <p class="eyebrow eyebrow-light">Getting started</p>
        <h2 class="section-heading section-heading-light">If you administer it.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="benefit-grid">
        <div class="benefit-item">
          <span class="benefit-numeral">I</span>
          <h3>Send out the link and code</h3>
          <p>Both came in your confirmation email. The link looks like <code>mereorthodoxy.com/join/?i=your-organization</code>, and the code is eight characters. Only people with an email address at your domain can use them.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="benefit-item">
          <span class="benefit-numeral">II</span>
          <h3>Put them where people look</h3>
          <p>A staff email, the intranet, a syllabus, the weekly bulletin. This is the step that decides whether any of the rest happens, and it is the one most often skipped.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="benefit-item">
          <span class="benefit-numeral">III</span>
          <h3>Add your other domains</h3>
          <p>Most organizations have more than one. A second campus or a legacy domain will not work until it is added. Do it on <a href="/institution-manage/">your organization page</a>.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="benefit-item">
          <span class="benefit-numeral">IV</span>
          <h3>Make sure you can Push</h3>
          <p>The Push button only renders for administrators. If you cannot find it, your account has not been made one yet. Email us and it takes a minute.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="benefit-item">
          <span class="benefit-numeral">V</span>
          <h3>See who has joined</h3>
          <p>Your organization page lists everyone signed up, so you know whether this is being used well before renewal comes around.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="benefit-item">
          <span class="benefit-numeral">VI</span>
          <h3>Renewal is a conversation</h3>
          <p>We reach out before your year is up. Nothing renews automatically and we keep no card on file.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      <div class="section-intro">
        <p class="eyebrow eyebrow-light">For your people</p>
        <h2 class="section-heading section-heading-light">If someone sent you a link.</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="benefit-grid">
        <div class="benefit-item">
          <span class="benefit-numeral">I</span>
          <h3>Use your work or school email</h3>
          <p>That address is what identifies you as covered. A personal account will not be recognised, even if it is the one you use for everything.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="benefit-item">
          <span class="benefit-numeral">II</span>
          <h3>Enter the code</h3>
          <p>Then you are finished. Nothing to pay, and no trial to remember to cancel.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="benefit-item">
          <span class="benefit-numeral">III</span>
          <h3>Signing in later</h3>
          <p>Use <strong>Sign in</strong> at the top of any page. We email you a link rather than asking you to invent another password.</p>
        </div>
      </div>

      {{!-- The "For librarians" section (no proxy / no IP ranges, LibGuide
           linking, course reserves, library print copies) was removed
           2026-08-19 at Ian's direction. If it comes back, it belongs after
           the "For your people" grid and before the closing capstone. --}}

      {{!-- 6 and 7. What is at stake, and what success looks like. --}}
      <div class="capstone">
        <p class="capstone-body">The people in your care will be formed either way. That is not in your control. What is in your control is whether anyone who loves them chose some of it on purpose, or whether it was all handed to them by whatever happened to be loudest that week.</p>
        <p class="capstone-body">Questions of any kind: <a href="mailto:ian@mereorthodoxy.com">ian@mereorthodoxy.com</a>. A person answers.</p>
        <p class="capstone-body"><a href="/institutions/">Back to organizational membership</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

</main>
